<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hopae</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hopae were identification tags carried by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> in the Joseon period, recording the bearer's name, birthdate, place of birth, status and residence, which helped the government collect taxes and recover runaway slaves. After certain military officials were required to wear them in 1391, King Taejong made them compulsory for all males in 1413 to control migration, but the law was dropped in 1416 once a new household registry was complete. The system was abolished and restored several times, revived by Sejo in 1458 and by Prince Gwanghae in the early 17th century. The tags marked class: yellow for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangban">yangban</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> or deer horn for officials, and wood for commoners and slaves.</p></div>
